00:00:33.070 --> 00:00:37.680
You’ve heard some worm horror stories, right?&nbsp;
We were looking some up just for this episode and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:40.576
came across a recent headline 
from ArsTechnica&nbsp;that read,&nbsp;

00:00:41.024 --> 00:00:48.267
“Army of worm larvae hatch from man’s&nbsp;
bum, visibly slither under his skin,”.

00:00:48.267 --> 00:00:52.480
And if that’s not enough to terrify you,
and make you feel very uncomfortable

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:57.440
there’s always the stories of painful stomach&nbsp;
cramps or diarrhea or nausea that eventually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.440 --> 00:01:02.854
turns out to be caused by some worms that have&nbsp;
taken up residence in someone’s intestines.&nbsp;

00:01:02.854 --> 00:01:08.240
It is terrifying and wild to think of something&nbsp;
so much smaller than us causing so much havoc.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:15.120
So, as we watch the cilia lining a worm’s gut&nbsp;
beat its own soothing pattern, wouldn’t it feel&nbsp;like,

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:21.280
almost like, a little bit of justice if this sight&nbsp;
wasn’t so peaceful? If worms had to worry about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:26.800
their own guts being taken over by a parasite?
If you’ve found yourself in this position,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:32.480
seeking schadenfreude over a worm, well we&nbsp;
have some good news for you. The worm you see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:38.284
in the middle of this tank is currently hosting&nbsp;
this strange fellow, called a Radiophyra.&nbsp;

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:46.400
James, our master of microscopes, had been on&nbsp;
the hunt for the Radiophyra after seeing this:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:52.560
two radiophyras linked together in a chain&nbsp;
as one divided to make more copies of itself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:56.800
It had come from the inside of one of&nbsp;
the worms he’d been watching, when he’d&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.800 --> 00:02:04.144
accidentally squeezed a worm a bit too hard under&nbsp;
the coverslip and caused the ciliate to pop out.&nbsp;

00:02:04.144 --> 00:02:08.160
Radiophyra belong to a general&nbsp;
group of ciliates called Astomes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:13.280
or astomatid ciliates. We’ve talked about ciliates&nbsp;
a lot on our channel, which means that if you’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:17.200
been watching us for a while, you may have&nbsp;
picked up on the fact that from time to time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:24.720
we have said that most ciliates have an oral groove,&nbsp;
that opening lined with cilia that sweep bacteria&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:30.800
and algae and other tiny bits of food into the&nbsp;
organism. We’ve seen that oral groove at work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:37.200
in ciliates like stentors and paramecium,&nbsp;
functioning as the ciliate equivalent of a mouth.&nbsp;

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:43.881
But as we have always said most ciliates, you 
will have inferred,  that this does not mean all ciliates.

00:02:43.881 --> 00:02:46.400
And if you are looking for an exception
to the rule,

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:53.040
astomes are that exception. Astomatid&nbsp;
ciliates are diverse, but they are unified by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:59.120
one shared feature, or rather, they are unified&nbsp;
by their lack of one shared feature, a mouth.&nbsp;

00:02:59.882 --> 00:03:06.302
And they don’t need a mouth because they&nbsp;
have something even better. They have worms.&nbsp;

00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:16.000
Astomatid ciliates do parasitize animals&nbsp;
other than worms. Some live inside mollusks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:22.240
others inside leeches or even in amphibians. But&nbsp;
they are most commonly associated with the guts of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.240 --> 00:03:29.120
annelid worms. So when James found his Radiophyra,&nbsp;
he decided to see if he could find more of them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:34.880
in the other worms that were in his samples. And&nbsp;
that meant that our master of microscopes had to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.880 --> 00:03:41.680
become a master of worm surgery, dissecting them&nbsp;
so he could draw out the ciliates living within.&nbsp;

00:03:42.320 --> 00:03:47.280
In–side this particular aquatic worm were&nbsp;
these astomatid ciliates. From a distance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:51.360
they also look like worms.
But as you get closer…&nbsp;

00:03:52.160 --> 00:03:58.960
And closer, their shape becomes more definite&nbsp;
except for the massive amounts of fluff around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:05.520
them, a dense cloud of cilia beating away.
Unfortunately, there isn’t a lot of research&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:10.800
on this ciliate. In fact, there isn’t a lot&nbsp;
of research on astomatid ciliates in general.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:16.080
They just aren’t destructive enough or common&nbsp;
enough to have become either a necessary or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.080 --> 00:04:21.600
convenient research subject. In fact, it’s&nbsp;
not even clear whether or not we should call&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:29.040
them parasites. Modern day papers will sometimes&nbsp;
refer to them endosymbionts instead, because we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:36.245
don't know a lot about whether astomatid ciliates&nbsp;
are doing much to their worm hosts, bad or good.&nbsp;

00:04:36.640 --> 00:04:41.680
But the worm gut does plenty for the&nbsp;
astomatid ciliates. At one point in time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:47.280
the ancestors of these ciliates did have&nbsp;
mouths. But as they found their way into worms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:52.160
and specifically their guts, those oral&nbsp;
grooves became less and less necessary.&nbsp;

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:59.840
Instead, the ciliates could rely on a form of&nbsp;
feeding called osmotrophy, where they simply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.840 --> 00:05:06.480
absorb nutrients from their surroundings through&nbsp;
osmosis. Instead of taking in larger bits of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:11.680
food through their mouths and breaking it down&nbsp;
themselves, astomatid ciliates could just take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:18.000
advantage of the worm’s digestive system to do&nbsp;
all that breaking down for them. As the worm’s&nbsp;

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:25.520
digestive enzymes&nbsp;break down complex molecules into 
simpler forms&nbsp; that can travel through their own intestinal walls,&nbsp;

00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:31.022
some of those nutrients would just go&nbsp;
feed the astomatid ciliate instead.&nbsp;

00:05:31.549 --> 00:05:36.560
These ciliates will actually sometimes be picky about&nbsp;
making sure they’re in a particular spot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.560 --> 00:05:41.200
within their host’s intestinal tract.&nbsp;
And once they’ve found the right spot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.200 --> 00:05:47.360
the ciliates hold themselves in place with&nbsp;
organelles that range in shape, some use hooks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.360 --> 00:05:53.600
other spines, or maybe even suckers. The flat&nbsp;
shape of the ciliates helps them stay pressed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.600 --> 00:05:59.200
the epithelium of the intestines. So while these&nbsp;
ciliates may not need a mouth anymore, they have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.200 --> 00:06:06.160
found other traits necessary to their survival.
Astomatid ciliates are found in hosts from all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.160 --> 00:06:11.920
sorts of environments. Some live in soil.&nbsp;
Some live in ponds. Some even live in ocean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.920 --> 00:06:18.560
waters. And scientists are using the general tools&nbsp;
available now to try and piece together how host&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.560 --> 00:06:24.000
and endosymbiont have shaped each other.&nbsp;
We can see some of that intertwined story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:28.960
in the ciliate’s mouthless-ness, but the&nbsp;
specifics of that evolutionary change&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.960 --> 00:06:34.400
are laid out in their DNA, as are the other&nbsp;
more hidden parts of that shared history.&nbsp;

00:06:35.120 --> 00:06:40.240
Now we cannot know whether worms have any&nbsp;
feelings about their guts being home&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.240 --> 00:06:45.840
to another organism. They likely don’t have much&nbsp;
choice in the matter, and they also likely aren’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.840 --> 00:06:51.520
prone to emotions like resentment the way we&nbsp;
might resent a worm parasitizing our bodies.&nbsp;

00:06:52.080 --> 00:06:56.080
But if they were to have any feelings on&nbsp;
the matter, we want to offer this one last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.080 --> 00:07:02.720
detail about astomatid ciliates: many of&nbsp;
them have their own endosymbionts as well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.720 --> 00:07:09.920
bacteria that live inside them with perhaps their&nbsp;
own history tangled up with their ciliate host’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.920 --> 00:07:15.280
story as well. Now we couldn’t find those&nbsp;
bacteria in our parasitic friends,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.280 --> 00:07:22.560
but we like the idea that somewhere out there are&nbsp;
these nesting dolls of endosymbiosis: an organism&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.560 --> 00:07:29.840
that is an organism but an organism that is also a&nbsp;
home buried within other homes. 
